WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.890
Imagine a scenario
where a bookstore has

2
00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:04.065
a database that
contains two tables.

3
00:00:04.065 --> 00:00:06.840
Customer table to track
customer information,

4
00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.720
and an order table to
track customers orders.

5
00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.130
But how can they determine

6
00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:13.245
which customer made which order?

7
00:00:13.245 --> 00:00:14.940
The solution is to add

8
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.970
a customer ID column into

9
00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:19.580
the order table column
as a foreign key.

10
00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:21.320
Over the next few minutes,

11
00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:22.700
you'll learn how to describe

12
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:24.240
the purpose of a foreign key,

13
00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.210
and demonstrate how to use it to

14
00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:29.015
connect different tables
in a relational database.

15
00:00:29.015 --> 00:00:31.405
What exactly is a foreign key?

16
00:00:31.405 --> 00:00:34.400
A foreign key is one or more
columns used to connect

17
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:35.870
two tables in order to

18
00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:37.940
create cross-referencing
between them.

19
00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:40.805
By foreign developers
mean external.

20
00:00:40.805 --> 00:00:42.800
The foreign key in one table,

21
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:44.450
we'll refer to an external or

22
00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:46.715
foreign column in another table.

23
00:00:46.715 --> 00:00:49.940
Let's find out more about
how foreign key works by

24
00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:51.020
exploring the tables from

25
00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:53.360
the database of an online store.

26
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.610
The store is customer
table contains

27
00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:58.465
information about the
customer's name and address.

28
00:00:58.465 --> 00:01:01.010
While their order table
contains information

29
00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:03.725
about each customer's
order date and status.

30
00:01:03.725 --> 00:01:06.665
The issue is how to connect
these tables to make

31
00:01:06.665 --> 00:01:08.030
sure that each order is

32
00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:09.935
associated with the
right customer.

33
00:01:09.935 --> 00:01:11.630
Establishing this connection is

34
00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:13.580
important so that
you can process,

35
00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:15.620
and deliver orders to
the right customers,

36
00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:19.475
update order details, or
cancel orders if required.

37
00:01:19.475 --> 00:01:21.590
A foreign key is a great method

38
00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:22.970
of establishing a relationship

39
00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:24.740
between these tables so that

40
00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:27.110
these other tasks
can be carried out.

41
00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:30.110
But before you learn about
how to use a foreign key,

42
00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:31.700
let's take a few
moments to explore

43
00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:33.850
the concept in a
bit more detail.

44
00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:36.830
To find out more about
how foreign keys work,

45
00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:38.810
let's take the example
of the relationship

46
00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:41.165
between two generic
entity tables.

47
00:01:41.165 --> 00:01:44.180
These tables are
called Table 1 or T1,

48
00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:47.035
and Table 2 or T2.

49
00:01:47.035 --> 00:01:50.210
The purpose of connecting
these tables is to relate

50
00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:51.650
records of data that exist

51
00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:53.615
in both tables with each other.

52
00:01:53.615 --> 00:01:55.550
The foreign key in T1

53
00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:58.085
should point to
related column in T2.

54
00:01:58.085 --> 00:02:01.775
In this case, the foreign
key column values in T1

55
00:02:01.775 --> 00:02:03.815
must correspond to
existing values

56
00:02:03.815 --> 00:02:06.155
in the reference column in T2.

57
00:02:06.155 --> 00:02:08.780
The reference column in T2 must

58
00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:11.435
contain unique values
in each row of data.

59
00:02:11.435 --> 00:02:14.775
This will most likely be
the primary column in T2.

60
00:02:14.775 --> 00:02:16.970
In addition, the reference table

61
00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:19.145
T2 is known as the parent table.

62
00:02:19.145 --> 00:02:22.610
While the referencing table
T1 is the child table.

63
00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:25.279
Don't worry if all this
seems a bit complicated.

64
00:02:25.279 --> 00:02:27.140
Let's simplify
things by exploring

65
00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:29.000
an entity relationship diagram

66
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.745
using the customer and
order tables from earlier.

67
00:02:31.745 --> 00:02:34.460
In this diagram, the
order table relates to

68
00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:36.170
the customer table by including

69
00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:38.060
the customer ID attribute,

70
00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:41.660
and defining it as foreign
key inside the order table.

71
00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:45.190
The relationship between these
two tables is one-to-many.

72
00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:46.790
You might have
encountered this type of

73
00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:48.650
relationship in
an earlier video.

74
00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:50.840
One-to-many means
that each customer

75
00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:52.130
may have many orders,

76
00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:55.505
but each order must refer to
one single customer only.

77
00:02:55.505 --> 00:02:58.250
This means there must
be a customer record

78
00:02:58.250 --> 00:02:59.795
available in the customer table

79
00:02:59.795 --> 00:03:01.465
before any order can be made.

80
00:03:01.465 --> 00:03:03.190
But it is not necessary to have

81
00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:05.470
an order once a new
customer is created.

82
00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:07.840
Therefore, the customer
table represents

83
00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:09.070
a parent table and

84
00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:11.660
the order table
represents a child table.

85
00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:14.070
This means that the
parent can exist,

86
00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:16.000
and the child may not exist.

87
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.760
But the opposite
scenario cannot occur.

88
00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:22.390
In this example, the customer
ID value existing in

89
00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:24.040
the order table can be used to

90
00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:26.290
fetch the records of
a specific customer,

91
00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:28.210
to determine who
placed the order.

92
00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:30.625
For example, to
generate an invoice

93
00:03:30.625 --> 00:03:33.320
or to deliver an order
through customer address.

94
00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:35.230
It is also possible for table

95
00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:37.255
to have more than
one foreign key.

96
00:03:37.255 --> 00:03:39.430
Each will be used to
connect the referencing or

97
00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:42.830
child table with other
referenced or parent tables.

98
00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:44.510
In this case, you'll have

99
00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:46.685
multiple parents
to the same child.

100
00:03:46.685 --> 00:03:48.245
Let's add a new table,

101
00:03:48.245 --> 00:03:50.330
product table into
the previous diagram

102
00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:52.565
to explain this in more detail.

103
00:03:52.565 --> 00:03:56.075
The order table now
has two foreign keys.

104
00:03:56.075 --> 00:03:57.830
One foreign key links it with

105
00:03:57.830 --> 00:04:00.215
the customer table
via the customer ID,

106
00:04:00.215 --> 00:04:01.460
and the other links it with

107
00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:03.800
the product table
via the product ID.

108
00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:07.175
The relationship between
these tables is one-to-one.

109
00:04:07.175 --> 00:04:10.820
Each order must be related to
a specific product record,

110
00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:12.950
and each product
might be related to

111
00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:15.260
an order record, but
doesn't have to be.

112
00:04:15.260 --> 00:04:16.970
For example, you can

113
00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:18.890
receive a new product
in your inventory,

114
00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:21.520
but no customer has placed
an order on it yet.

115
00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:24.125
If an order has not been
placed in this product,

116
00:04:24.125 --> 00:04:26.630
then it's not related
to any order yet.

117
00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:28.820
This then raises the question,

118
00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:31.520
who is the parent,
and who is the child?

119
00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:34.865
The customer, the
order, or the product?

120
00:04:34.865 --> 00:04:37.670
The answer is that there
are now two parents,

121
00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:39.770
the customer and
the product tables,

122
00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:42.695
and there's one child,
which is the order table.

123
00:04:42.695 --> 00:04:45.920
You should now understand the
purpose of a foreign key,

124
00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:47.840
and you should also be
able to demonstrate

125
00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:48.890
how it can be used to

126
00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:53.070
connect tables in a relational
database. Well done.